    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nWhen Visual put replaces all lines, the leftover empty line is deleted with `ml_delete_flags()` and only `on_lines` is sent: no `on_bytes`, marks are not adjusted, and callbacks see the cursor on the deleted line. When the put fails, a non-existent line is \"deleted\", which sets 'modified' and bumps changedtick.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nDelete the line with `del_lines()`, and skip it when the buffer is still empty.\r\n\r\n",
    "closed_at": null,
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "did you notice this because you are working on a plugin or client that ran into this?\r\n\r\nAI findings regarding this PR:\r\n\r\nThe normal case works: `ggVG\"ap` now emits a correct `on_bytes` event for the deleted leftover line. Issues, all around `normal.c:6676-6680`:\r\n\r\n1. **A failed put deletes a line that doesn't exist.** The branch only checks that the buffer was emptied and the last line is blank, so it also runs when the put inserts nothing. Example: buffer `{'x', 'y'}`, empty register `z`,\r\n`ggVG\"zp`:\r\n   - The delete leaves an empty buffer (`ML_EMPTY`), and the put fails with E353.\r\n   - `ml_delete_flags(1)` just replaces `''` with `''` (still 1 line).\r\n   - The new `extmark_adjust()` then emits `on_bytes` deleting row 1 of a 1-row buffer, and records that in undo.\r\n   - After `u`, an `on_bytes` shadow (treesitter, or this spec's verify harness) would have 5 bytes while the buffer has 4. (I got this from reading the code, not by running this branch.)\r\n   - Other triggers: `\"%p` with no file name, `\":p` before any cmdline, an empty `'nomodifiable'` buffer.\r\n\r\n   Suggested fix: skip the block when `curbuf->b_ml.ml_flags & ML_EMPTY`.\r\n\r\n2. **Older bug in the same branch** (not introduced here, but the same check fixes it): in a fresh empty buffer, a failed Visual put (`V\"zp` with an empty register, or `V\"ap` with `'nomodifiable'`) calls `deleted_lines(2, 1)`. That fires\r\n`on_lines` for a 1-line buffer, sets `'modified'` (even when `'nomodifiable'`), bumps `changedtick`, and sets `'.` to line 2 of a 1-line buffer.\r\n\r\n3. **Callbacks see the cursor on the deleted line.** With `{'x', 'y'}` and a linewise register `a`, `ggVG\"agp` leaves the cursor on line 3 (the leftover). The new `on_bytes` event, like the existing `on_lines`, fires before the cursor is\r\nfixed up, so a callback that calls `nvim_get_current_line()` or `col('.')` reads past the end of the buffer. `del_lines()` moves the cursor before notifying to avoid exactly this.\r\n\r\n4. **The return value of `ml_delete_flags()` is ignored.** If the delete fails, `on_bytes` still reports a deleted row, and undo records it.\r\n\r\n5. **`extmark_adjust()` + `deleted_lines()` re-implements `deleted_lines_mark()`, minus its `mark_adjust()`.** So folds, diff blocks and quickfix entries aren't told about the deleted line.\r\n\r\n6. **Root cause:** the raw `ml_delete_flags()` means each notification (`on_lines` before, `on_bytes` now, marks still missing) has to be added by hand at this call site. Deleting through `del_lines()` would cover undo, the cursor fix-up\r\nbefore callbacks, and marks/extmarks, and would fix (3). Currently the deletion is only undone correctly because `u_getbot()` infers it from the line-count change.\r\n\r\n7. **Tests:** the new test only covers a put that succeeds. Adding `ggVG\"zp` with an empty register, then `u`, to this `do_both(verify)` test should fail the byte-count check and catch (1). Redo (`<C-r>`), `gp`, and extmark positions\r\naren't covered either. Consider extending the existing \"visual paste\" tests instead of adding a new case.\r\n",
